module v4.extdeps.languages.rust


import v4.std.node { Node, Symbol }
// Bool from v4.std.logic is KERNEL-AMBIENT (STRUCTURE.md "Kernel-
// ambient types") — usable without an import statement in any v4
// .dag file. The live `IrToRust<Bool>` row below references the
// type-parameter `Bool` directly; v2-compiler resolves it ambiently
// (verified 0-diag).


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Modeling notes (read before validating against the anchor)
//
// THE SLICE-VS-BUNDLED FRAMING (T-4, manager directive msg_4f7e1280,
// operator-critical-path item 3). T-4 is the bundled work across the
// 5 Shape A source languages: "the SHAPE is the work" — establish the
// canonical declarative LanguageModel shape on one language, then fan
// out to the other 4. THIS slice is the rust HALF of fix-the-shape-
// once: the type-system VOCABULARY and Rust-side carrier shapes, with
// the D1/D2-dependent pieces (parse/emit operations + scalar algebra
// inhabitance instance-values) explicitly DEFERRED to their named
// owners. Deferral ≠ improvisation: every deferred item is named in
// "Owned ELSEWHERE" with its owner and dissolution trigger.
//
// THE RUST → SUBSTRATE STRUCTURAL FIT. Each Rust surface concept maps
// to a member of the closed substrate axes (node.dag's 6 connectives
// + 5 L1 behaviors). The mapping, member-by-member — recording it
// here because the fit IS the deliverable (per the slice's structural
// finding above):
//
//   - FUNCTION declarations and applications split across the two
//     substrate axes per A1's two-coordinated-substrate framing:
//
//     A Rust `fn name(args) -> T { body }` DECLARATION ↦ Arrow
//     (the type-substrate connective; node.dag's TypeNode with
//     connective: Arrow). The declaration introduces a typed
//     function — `(args) -> T` — whose body is a sub-DAG of L1
//     behaviors (Value / Transform / Branch / Loop / Bind),
//     referenced from the Arrow's children. The function's
//     IDENTITY (a FunctionRef the call site refers to) is the
//     Arrow declaration.
//
//     A Rust function APPLICATION (call site `f(x, y)`) ↦ Transform
//     (the computation-substrate behavior; node.dag's
//     ComputationNode with behavior: Transform). Transform IS the
//     application behavior of the typed-total-λ fragment per
//     INVARIANTS:72 (Curry-Howard / System F lineage): the callee
//     is a function value (resolved to the Arrow it references),
//     the args are operands, and the application yields the
//     result. node.dag:209 names this explicitly: "Transform =
//     application."
//
//     The distinction matters per the substrate's
//     two-coordinated-axes discipline (THESIS §198-203): a Node
//     belongs to EXACTLY one substrate at a time — either type-
//     side (with a connective) or computation-side (with a
//     behavior). A `fn` declaration and its call site are
//     DIFFERENT Nodes: the declaration is a type Node (Arrow
//     connective), the call site is a computation Node (Transform
//     behavior). Conflating them was the earlier doc's defect,
//     surfaced by openai-pro REQUEST_CHANGES 2026-05-16T11:15:38Z.
//
//   - LET-BINDING (`let x: T = expr;` and `let mut x: T = expr;`) ↦
//     Bind. A Rust `let` introduces a name into a scope with a value;
//     that is exactly Bind (node.dag:209 "Bind = let"). Mutability of
//     the binding (`mut`) is a structural BINDING-LEVEL fact (the
//     binding admits later `x = …;`), distinct from the REFERENCE-
//     KIND-LEVEL mutability `&mut`; the latter is a TYPE-LEVEL fact
//     carried by RustReferenceKind. The two are not the same axis,
//     per the Rust Reference's separation of place expressions and
//     reference types.
//
//   - MATCH (`match expr { pat => … }`) ↦ Branch. Rust's match against
//     a sum-typed value (`Option<T>` / `Result<T, E>` / user `enum`s)
//     is exactly Branch over the Disj's variants (node.dag:209
//     "Branch = sums"). The match is total-by-construction (Rust
//     itself rejects non-exhaustive matches over closed enums); that
//     totality IS Branch's discipline. Predicated execution
//     (`if cond { … } else { … }`) folds into the same Branch over a
//     two-variant `Bool` Disj.
//
//   - LOOP (`for x in iter`, `while cond`, `loop { … break …; }`) ↦
//     Loop with BOUNDED descent (node.dag:42-68, the A2 discipline).
//     `for x in iter` is bounded by the iterator's cardinality (a
//     finite measure when the iterator is bounded); `while cond` is
//     bounded by a ranking dimension that decreases on each
//     iteration; `loop { }` is structurally unbounded UNLESS the
//     body's `break` makes the effective descent bounded. A Rust
//     `loop {}` with no break is a STOP under A2 (the substrate
//     refuses to claim totality it cannot prove); the v4 Rust model
//     reads bounded loops, fail-closes on unbounded ones.
//
//   - LITERAL / VALUE (`42i32`, `"hello"`, `true`) ↦ Value
//     (node.dag:209). A literal IS a Value. The literal's typed
//     interpretation (its scalar kind + width for numerics; its
//     UTF-8 byte sequence for strings) is the inhabitance walk that
//     RIDES D2 — pre-D2 the Value's TYPED interpretation is
//     unspecified; the Value carrier itself is just a Node. (`()` is
//     a Value literal too, but it is a zero-tuple TupleType — out-of-
//     scope for this slice; see SCOPE NEGATIVE Tuple types below.)
//
//   - REFERENCES (`&T`, `&mut T`, `'a` lifetime parameters) ↦ effect-
//     typed parameter (structural Conj field), per THESIS:401 IN-B
//     applied to Rust. A reference is NOT a separate behavior; it is
//     a TYPE whose `kind: RustReferenceKind` and `lifetime: Symbol`
//     fields are structural Conj coordinates. Memory access through
//     a reference is a Transform whose operand type CARRIES the
//     reference kind in its signature; the borrow-checker (T-13
//     ownership lens) READS that signature and validates well-
//     scopedness as a structural property. This is EXACTLY the ptx
//     IN-B precedent (`state_space: StateSpace` on RegisterScalar);
//     references are Rust's analog of state-space tags.
//     Tempting alternative: a `Borrow` behavior wrapping every `&T`
//     formation. REJECTED — that would parallel-represent what the
//     Transform's signature already says; the effect-typed parameter
//     is the unannotated primitive (feedback_no_annotations).
//
// CONCLUSION. The type-system half of Rust's spec fits the existing
// substrate with no new connectives, no new behaviors, no parallel
// taxonomies. The classifiers declared below are the closed Rust-side
// surface; their composition with grammar productions (Owned
// ELSEWHERE) and inhabitance instance-values (Owned ELSEWHERE — rides
// D2) lands when those dependencies dissolve, with the I/O contract
// of THIS file immutable.
//
// SCOPE NEGATIVE (operator-tier, do not work around).
//   - Tuple types (`(T₁, T₂, …)`, including the zero-tuple `()` /
//     "unit"). Per the Rust Reference §types-tuple, tuples are their
//     own type category — NOT primitive scalars. `()` is the zero-
//     field TupleType (the single-inhabitant terminal type by
//     CARDINALITY, not a scalar primitive). Modeling tuples requires
//     a generic tuple-type carrier (Conj over `arity: Cardinality`
//     and `components: List<Type>`), which composes over
//     std/cardinality.dag's refinement substrate (T-3) when that
//     lands. Until then `()` and tuple types in general are out-of-
//     scope for this slice; emitting `()` as a literal Value is
//     fine (it is a Value Node — the modeling notes' LITERAL/VALUE
//     mapping says so) but the type carrier is deferred to the
//     tuple-types substrate.
//     (Corrected 2026-05-16 per BLOCKING operator review on PR
//     #3174: the prior slice mis-classified `()` as a `Unit` variant
//     of `RustScalarKind`. The Reference partitions it as a tuple,
//     and INVARIANTS P1 modeling-faithfulness requires the substrate
//     partition to ground in the spec partition. Removed from this
//     slice; named for tuple-types substrate.)
//   - The full Rust GRAMMAR production set (item declarations,
//     expression syntax, pattern syntax, attribute syntax, macro
//     syntax) is owned by the bundled T-4 LanguageModel shape — same
//     framing as ptx's "warp-level intrinsics + tensor cores"
//     scope-negative. Not authored here.
//   - Rust async/await semantics (`async fn`, `.await`, `Future`
//     trait). These compose over the type-system carriers declared
//     here (an `async fn` returns a Future-typed value), but the
//     coordination-effect typing is owned by extdeps/coordination.dag
//     (T-4.8, IN-B carriers); not authored here.
//   - Trait objects (`dyn Trait`), generic bounds (`<T: Bound>`),
//     associated types — Rust's nominal generics + bounded
//     polymorphism. Modeled at the LanguageModel grammar layer (T-4
//     bundled work); structural inhabitance against std/algebra.dag
//     algebras rides D2. The reference Conj record below carries an
//     opaque `lifetime: Symbol` field — that IS the generic-lifetime-
//     parameter surface; richer trait-bound surfaces compose over the
//     LanguageModel.
//   - Const generics, type-level integers, `[T; N]` array length as a
//     generic. Modeled at the LanguageModel layer; the `N` length
//     surfaces as a bounded `Int` via std/cardinality.dag refinement
//     when that lands.
//   - `unsafe` blocks, raw pointers (`*const T` / `*mut T`), FFI. The
//     `unsafe` surface is a STRUCTURAL boundary fact (the function /
//     block's signature carries an `unsafe` discriminator); the
//     ownership lens (T-13) reads that fact to admit operations the
//     safe surface forbids. The discriminator carrier lands when
//     T-13's brief is authored; not the rust-vocabulary layer.
//
// PRACTICE-4 LEDGER DISCIPLINE. Every closed-Disj carrier below
// carries the full 🟢/🟡/🔴 + all five patterns (Fact-placement /
// Variant-is-data / Algebraic-form / Dimensional / Parameterized-
// family-Practice-7) per modeling-discipline.md §4 (operator-ratified
// binding 2026-05-15, gate (b) — #3163 made the 4-pattern miss the
// proof case; do not omit Practice-7). Conj records carry no ledger
// (only coproducts dissolve); scaffold-residual Conj fields (RustCost
// raw Int components) carry a 🟡 bridge note mirroring std/diagnostic
// .dag's `Extent.ByteRange` and ptx.dag's `Dim3` / `PtxCost`
// precedent.


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// RustIntKind — the closed Rust signed-vs-unsigned integer kind. The
// Rust Reference §3.4 numeric-types-integer-types declares exactly
// two integer kinds: signed (i*) and unsigned (u*). Each integer
// scalar in Rust's spec is one of these.
// Spec: https://doc.rust-lang.org/reference/types/numeric.html
//
//   - Signed   — `i8` / `i16` / `i32` / `i64` / `i128` / `isize`
//                (two's-complement signed integers)
//   - Unsigned — `u8` / `u16` / `u32` / `u64` / `u128` / `usize`
//                (unsigned modular integers)
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). A third integer kind (e.g. a saturating-
// integer family distinct from the existing wrapping `Wrapping<T>`
// library type) is an edit to THIS declaration routed to operator
// ratification under the L-2 pin discipline (a future Reference
// revision modifying the integer kind partition is a visible diff,
// never a silent extension). The `Wrapping<T>` / `Saturating<T>`
// library types are LIBRARIES (per L-2, a library is just a program
// in the modeled language = Node), NOT additional integer kinds.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every integer-scalar consumer (the
//      Rust `LanguageModel` grammar's integer-literal production,
//      the per-target cost-realization for integer ops, the
//      eventual inhabitance walk for Semiring-vs-OrderedRing
//      dispatch) reads the kind tag uniformly; shared classifier,
//      not a per-consumer scattered fact.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: nullary tags from a closed
//      Reference-defined set. A `{ signed: Bool }` proxy replaces a
//      closed 2-valued exhaustive sum with an unbounded Bool — and
//      while Bool is 2-valued, the Bool proxy ALSO admits the read
//      "is the integer kind 'true' or 'false'?" — exactly the M4
//      "closed sets are enums, not numeric/boolean proxies" smell.
//      The enum IS the faithful form.
//   3. Algebraic form — PARTIAL: each kind grounds in a std/algebra
//      structure (Signed → OrderedRing, Unsigned → Semiring — minus
//      negate). That grounding IS the inhabitance walk owned by
//      std/integer.dag per P2 (and rides D2 + T-3 per "Owned
//      ELSEWHERE"); the kind enum itself stays a closed classifier.
//      The "kind → algebra" map is the future inhabitance walk, not
//      a richer std/ source to collapse this enum INTO.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single Rust integer type has EXACTLY
//      ONE kind; `i32` is signed, `u32` is unsigned, the Reference
//      admits no "both" type. Mutually-exclusive alternatives, not
//      simultaneous coordinates. (Kind × Width is the Cartesian
//      product on RustIntScalar's IntScalar variant — that is the
//      dimensional model.)
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: Signed and Unsigned are the
//      Rust Reference's integer-kind partition; they are NOT one
//      generic `F<X>` / projection over a separately-declared source
//      set (Practice 7). One could attempt `IntKind<algebra:
//      AlgebraStructure>` parameterized over a 2-element algebra
//      subset, but the partition is the spec's irreducible
//      kind-distinction, not a mechanical copy of a richer set.
// Terminal: the closed two-way Rust integer-kind partition; a 3rd is
// the C1 stop-signal (a visible ratified edit to this declaration,
// STRUCTURE.md commitment #1).
type RustIntKind
  = Signed
  | Unsigned


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// RustIntWidth — the closed Rust integer bit-width classifier. The
// Rust Reference §3.4 admits exactly six widths in the stable spec
// (each available in both Signed and Unsigned kind, except Pointer
// which is platform-dependent and reads the host machine's
// pointer-width per std/machine.dag).
// Spec: https://doc.rust-lang.org/reference/types/numeric.html
//
//   - Bits8    — `i8`  / `u8`     (1-byte integers)
//   - Bits16   — `i16` / `u16`    (2-byte integers)
//   - Bits32   — `i32` / `u32`    (4-byte integers; the Rust idiomatic
//                                  default integer width)
//   - Bits64   — `i64` / `u64`    (8-byte integers)
//   - Bits128  — `i128` / `u128`  (16-byte integers)
//   - Pointer  — `isize` / `usize` (platform pointer-sized integers;
//                                   the host's pointer-width per
//                                   std/machine.dag PointerWidth)
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). A 256-bit width is an edit to THIS
// declaration under the L-2 pin discipline (a future Reference
// revision adding an integer width is a visible diff).
//
// Pointer is STRUCTURALLY distinct from the fixed bit-counts — it
// reads the host machine's pointer-width per STRUCTURE.md's
// PointerWidth platform-dependence rule (commitment #2 referenced
// indirectly: scalar widths declare their platform-dependence
// structurally, not as an annotation). When std/machine.dag's
// PointerWidth carrier lands (T-3 wave A3 dependency cited in
// "Owned ELSEWHERE"), Pointer-width Rust integers resolve through
// it; pre-T-3 the Pointer variant is the declared SURFACE that the
// resolution will plug into. This is NOT a "scaffold" 🟡 bridge —
// the variant is the terminal form; only its dependent inhabitance
// rides T-3.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every integer-scalar consumer reads
//      the width to size representations (emission of `i32` vs
//      `i64`; per-target cost-realization sizes); shared
//      classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: a `{ bits: Int }` proxy
//      (8/16/32/64/128/<pointer-width>) replaces a closed 6-valued
//      exhaustive sum with an unbounded Int (bits=42
//      representable), AND fails to express Pointer's platform-
//      dependence as a structural fact (it would force a single
//      concrete bit-count at decl time). The widths are NOT free
//      Int values; they are the Rust-Reference-admitted subset, so
//      a free-Int carrier would ratify illegal widths (the
//      INVARIANTS P2 illegal-states-unrepresentable shape, same as
//      ptx.dag RegisterWidth).
//   3. Algebraic form — partially N/A: the bit-counts form an
//      ordered set (8 < 16 < 32 < … with Pointer ∈ {32, 64} on
//      common targets), but the ORDER is derived FROM the closed
//      enum (and Pointer's value reads machine.dag), not a richer
//      source — pointing to a `Lattice<RustIntWidth>` would
//      parallel-represent the enumeration the order is defined
//      over (algebra.dag U1 engine-debt smell).
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single typed integer has EXACTLY ONE
//      width; mutually-exclusive alternatives. The kind × width
//      Cartesian product lives on RustScalar's IntScalar variant
//      (Disj-with-payload), not on this enum.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the six widths are the Rust
//      Reference's stable-integer-width set; they are NOT one
//      generic `F<X>` / projection over a separately-declared
//      source set (Practice 7). The bit-count itself IS the
//      discriminant for the fixed variants; Pointer is a
//      DIFFERENT-SHAPED variant (platform-dependent, not a fixed
//      count) — they are not all `F<bit-count: Int>`. A richer
//      machine-width source would be std/machine.dag's
//      MachineWidth, but that source is the inhabitance walk's
//      target (Compose<Int, MachineWidth<Word*>>), not a
//      collapsing source for this enum.
// Terminal: the closed six-way Rust integer-width classifier for the
// pinned Rust Reference; a 7th is the C1 stop-signal.
type RustIntWidth
  = Bits8
  | Bits16
  | Bits32
  | Bits64
  | Bits128
  | Pointer


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// RustFloatWidth — the closed Rust floating-point bit-width
// classifier. The Rust Reference §3.4 numeric-types-floating-point
// stable spec admits exactly two widths (both IEEE-754 binary
// formats); f16 and f128 exist in the Reference as UNSTABLE feature
// gates and are NOT part of the pinned stable spec this file models
// (they are a visible C1 edit when they stabilize, never a silent
// extension).
// Spec: https://doc.rust-lang.org/reference/types/numeric.html
//
//   - Bits32 — `f32`  (IEEE-754 binary32 single-precision)
//   - Bits64 — `f64`  (IEEE-754 binary64 double-precision; the Rust
//                      idiomatic default float width)
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). f16 / bf16 / f128 stabilization is an
// edit to THIS declaration under the L-2 pin discipline.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every float-scalar consumer reads the
//      width to size representations (emission of `f32` vs `f64`;
//      cost-realization for floating-point ops); shared classifier,
//      not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: a `{ bits: Int }` proxy (32/64)
//      replaces a closed 2-valued exhaustive sum with an unbounded
//      Int — and floating-point widths are NOT free Int values;
//      they are the IEEE-754-binary-format-admitted subset Rust
//      stabilizes, so a free-Int carrier would ratify illegal
//      widths (P2 illegal-states-unrepresentable; the same shape
//      as RustIntWidth's argument).
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: the two widths
//      structurally inhabit the SAME algebra (std/algebra.dag
//      `ApproximateField` per the rounding-aware framing —
//      INVARIANTS:43, std/float.dag scaffold's anchor); they do
//      not partition into distinct algebraic structures (`f32` and
//      `f64` are both ApproximateFields, only the precision
//      differs). No richer std/ source to collapse the enum INTO.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single typed float has EXACTLY ONE
//      width; mutually-exclusive alternatives. (Rust does not
//      admit `f32 ⊓ f64` as a hybrid type.)
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the two widths are the Rust-
//      stable-IEEE-754-binary-format set; they are NOT one generic
//      `F<X>` / projection over a separately-declared source set
//      (Practice 7). A richer IEEE-754 source (the full
//      binary16/32/64/128 set) is the spec's universe, but Rust
//      admits only the binary32/64 subset stably — the choice of
//      "which widths Rust stabilizes" is the partition discriminant,
//      not a parameterization of a richer source set.
// Terminal: the closed two-way Rust stable-float-width classifier.
type RustFloatWidth
  = Bits32
  | Bits64


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// RustScalarKind — the closed Rust scalar-type kind classifier. The
// Rust Reference §3 partitions the non-compound primitive types into
// exactly six kinds, per §3.3 boolean-type, §3.4 numeric-types (the
// Int / Float bifurcation), §3.5 textual-types (the Char / Str
// bifurcation), and §3.6 never-type:
// Spec: https://doc.rust-lang.org/reference/types.html
//   Bool:    https://doc.rust-lang.org/reference/types/boolean.html
//   Int:     https://doc.rust-lang.org/reference/types/numeric.html
//   Float:   https://doc.rust-lang.org/reference/types/numeric.html
//   Char:    https://doc.rust-lang.org/reference/types/textual.html
//   Str:     https://doc.rust-lang.org/reference/types/textual.html
//   Never:   https://doc.rust-lang.org/reference/types/never.html
//
//   - Int      — integer scalars (Reference §3.4 numeric-types-
//                integer-types; both signed and unsigned, all
//                widths — see RustIntKind × RustIntWidth)
//   - Float    — floating-point scalars (Reference §3.4
//                numeric-types-floating-point; f32 / f64 stable —
//                see RustFloatWidth)
//   - Bool     — the `bool` two-valued type (Reference §3.3
//                boolean-type; classical-logic-inhabiting per
//                std/logic.dag)
//   - Char     — the `char` Unicode scalar value type (Reference
//                §3.5 textual-types; 32-bit Unicode code-point,
//                strictly excluding the surrogate range)
//   - Str      — the `str` UTF-8 string-slice type (Reference §3.5
//                textual-types; a DYNAMICALLY-SIZED TYPE (DST), per
//                Reference §dynamically-sized-types). DSTs appear in
//                a broader set of legal contexts than just references:
//                behind reference types (`&str` / `&mut str`); behind
//                pointer types (`*const str` / `*mut str` / `Box<str>`
//                / `Rc<str>` / `Arc<str>`); as `?Sized` type parameters
//                / associated types; and as the FINAL field of a
//                struct (`struct S { header: u32, data: str }`). The
//                unsized / DST property is a STRUCTURAL Rust fact,
//                separate from the kind classification here; a
//                sized-vs-unsized substrate fact (and the full DST
//                composition surface) is a future refinement carrier
//                — for this slice the kind enum closes the type-
//                classification axis. Downstream consumers reading a
//                Str-kinded scalar are obligated to compose it with
//                one of the legal DST contexts; reference is the most
//                common but not the only valid one.)
//   - Never    — the `!` never type (Reference §3.6 never-type; the
//                empty-inhabitance type a diverging computation
//                returns. `!` IS in the pinned Reference's primitive-
//                types section, so per L-2 "model the spec" it
//                belongs in the partition. Specific in-position
//                stability gates are a Reference detail, not a
//                grounds for omitting the kind from the partition;
//                the inhabitance of `!` rides D2 + the future
//                empty-cardinality carrier per `std/cardinality.dag`)
//
// `()` (the unit type) is DELIBERATELY NOT in this partition: the
// Reference §3 partitions it as a TupleType (the zero-field tuple),
// not a primitive scalar. Tuple types — including `()` — are
// out-of-scope for this slice; see SCOPE NEGATIVE Tuple types in
// the header modeling notes. (Corrected 2026-05-16 per BLOCKING
// operator review on PR #3174: the prior slice's `Unit` variant
// mis-classified `()` as a primitive-scalar kind. INVARIANTS P1
// modeling-faithfulness requires the partition to ground in the
// spec's partition; the Reference puts `()` in §3.10 TupleType, so
// it cannot be a RustScalarKind variant under this discipline.)
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). A seventh scalar kind is an edit to
// THIS declaration under the L-2 pin discipline. The kind enum is
// the meta-classifier; the typed scalar values themselves are the
// `RustScalar` Disj below (which carries the kind × width
// admissibility as structural variant payloads).
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every Rust-typed value consumer
//      branches on the scalar kind to dispatch (which algebra
//      applies, which width is meaningful, which emission syntax
//      to use); shared classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: a `{ name: Symbol }` proxy
//      reopens the readable-string heuristic surface K-1 forecloses
//      (a `match name { "i32" => … }` smell, exactly the v3
//      regression the K-1 opacity discipline structurally
//      prevents). The enum IS the faithful form.
//   3. Algebraic form — PARTIAL: each kind grounds in a std/algebra
//      structure (Int → OrderedRing-or-Semiring per
//      RustIntKind, Float → ApproximateField, Bool →
//      BooleanAlgebra, Char → no exact arithmetic algebra by
//      design — Char's algebra is the surrogate-excluded subset
//      of Int32 cardinality, Str → FreeMonoid<Char> per the Rust
//      Reference's UTF-8 textual semantics — owned by
//      std/text.dag's eventual inhabitance, Never → terminal
//      empty-cardinality, owned by std/cardinality.dag). That
//      grounding is the INHABITANCE walk owned by the std/ scalar
//      files per P2 (rides D2 + T-3 wave A3). The kind enum itself
//      stays a closed classifier; the "kind → algebra" map IS the
//      future inhabitance walk, not a richer std/ source to
//      collapse this enum INTO. Same shape as ptx.dag
//      RegisterScalarKind.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single Rust scalar value has EXACTLY
//      ONE kind; mutually-exclusive alternatives. (Kind × Width is
//      a Cartesian product on RustScalar's IntScalar / FloatScalar
//      variants — that IS the dimensional model for the
//      width-bearing kinds.)
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the six kinds are the Rust-
//      Reference primitive-type partition; they are NOT one generic
//      `F<X>` / projection over a separately-declared source set
//      (Practice 7). One could attempt `RustScalarKind<algebra:
//      AlgebraStructure>` parameterized over an algebra-structure
//      subset, but the kinds ground in STRUCTURALLY HETEROGENEOUS
//      algebraic structures (OrderedRing/Semiring/ApproximateField/
//      BooleanAlgebra/FreeMonoid/empty-cardinality — five distinct
//      std/algebra structures plus one cardinality fact), and Char
//      grounds in a Cardinality refinement not an arithmetic
//      algebra. The partition is the spec's irreducible primitive-
//      kind distinction, not a copy of any enumerable source.
// Terminal: the closed six-way Rust primitive-scalar-kind
// classifier; `()` is TupleType (Reference §3.10), out-of-scope
// for this slice.
type RustScalarKind
  = Int
  | Float
  | Bool
  | Char
  | Str
  | Never


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// RustScalar — a typed Rust primitive-scalar value: kind × (width if
// applicable). Disj sum with NAMED-FIELD VARIANT PAYLOADS so that
// the kind × width admissibility is STRUCTURALLY ENFORCED — Bool /
// Char / Str / Never carry no width payload, so "Bool with width=
// Bits32" is unrepresentable (not "rejected by a check" —
// unrepresentable, the INVARIANTS P2 illegal-states-unrepresentable
// shape, the same design choice node.dag took with EdgeLabel's
// `Positional` and algebra.dag took with the Field type's omitted
// total `reciprocal`).
//
//   - IntScalar    — { kind: RustIntKind, width: RustIntWidth }
//                    e.g. IntScalar { kind: Signed, width: Bits32 }
//                    is Rust's `i32`; IntScalar { kind: Unsigned,
//                    width: Pointer } is `usize`.
//   - FloatScalar  — { width: RustFloatWidth }
//                    e.g. FloatScalar { width: Bits64 } is `f64`.
//   - BoolScalar   — Rust's `bool` (the two-valued type — there is
//                    no width variant; the Reference does not expose
//                    bool's storage width as a discriminator).
//   - CharScalar   — Rust's `char` (Unicode scalar value; the
//                    32-bit representation is internal storage, not
//                    a kind-level discriminator the Reference
//                    exposes).
//   - StrScalar    — Rust's `str` (UTF-8 unsized string slice;
//                    Reference §3.5 textual-types). A DYNAMICALLY-
//                    SIZED TYPE (DST) per Reference §dynamically-
//                    sized-types — legal in any DST context: behind
//                    reference types (`&str` / `&mut str`); behind
//                    pointer types (`*const str` / `*mut str` /
//                    `Box<str>` / `Rc<str>` / `Arc<str>`); as
//                    `?Sized` type parameters / associated types;
//                    as the final field of a struct. References are
//                    the most common context but not the only valid
//                    one. The unsized / DST property is a STRUCTURAL
//                    Rust fact; there is no kind-level width or sub-
//                    discriminator on str itself).
//   - NeverScalar  — Rust's `!` (Reference §3.6 never-type; empty-
//                    inhabitance type. No width, no kind sub-
//                    discriminator; its empty-cardinality is the
//                    grounding fact, deferred to std/cardinality
//                    .dag's empty-cardinality carrier per "Owned
//                    ELSEWHERE").
//
// `()` (the zero-tuple Unit type) is DELIBERATELY NOT a variant of
// this Disj: per Reference §3.10 it is a TupleType, not a primitive
// scalar. See SCOPE NEGATIVE Tuple types in the header.
// Spec: https://doc.rust-lang.org/reference/types.html
//   (Per-variant Spec URLs on RustScalarKind above; this Disj is the
//   typed-value carrier composing kind × width over those.)
//
// The structural argument for the Disj-with-payloads (vs ptx's
// Conj-record `RegisterScalar { kind, width, state_space }`): in
// PTX every typed register has a (kind, width, state_space), so
// the three-coordinate Conj is the natural form. In Rust, the
// six scalar kinds have DIFFERENT structural shapes — Int has
// (kind, width), Float has (width), Bool/Char/Str/Never have
// nothing beyond the kind. A Conj record `{ kind, width? }` would
// force a width Option for Bool/Char/Str/Never, ratifying the
// illegal state "Bool with width=Bits32" — exactly the M4 /
// Practice-2/6 / P2 pattern those disciplines structurally forbid.
// The Disj-with-payloads is the dissolved form: each variant
// carries EXACTLY the structural data its kind admits, and no
// illegal kind×width combination is constructible.
//
// SEAM (where D2 inhabitance plugs in). When the D2 form decision
// lands (operator-pending, msg_640d429c), the per-variant
// inhabitance instance-values land in this file with these typed
// variants as their carriers — IntScalar { kind: Signed, width:
// Bits32 } is the carrier for Rust's i32 inhabitance into
// OrderedRing (or its v4-equivalent per the D2-ratified form);
// FloatScalar { width: Bits32 } is the carrier for f32 →
// ApproximateField; StrScalar is the carrier for `str` →
// FreeMonoid<Char>; NeverScalar is the carrier for `!` → empty-
// cardinality. The seam itself is the typed variant; the
// inhabitance value is the deferred row.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every Rust-typed value consumer
//      (literal parsing, expression typing, emission to source,
//      cost-realization, eventual inhabitance dispatch) reads the
//      scalar's kind + width-if-applicable through this carrier;
//      shared, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: collapsing the variants to a
//      uniform record `{ kind: RustScalarKind, width: Option<…> }`
//      reintroduces the illegal state "Bool with width=Bits32"
//      this Disj-with-payloads design exists to forbid. The
//      six-variant Disj IS the dissolved form (P2 + Practice
//      2/6 + M4).
//   3. Algebraic form — PARTIAL: each variant grounds in a std/
//      algebra or cardinality structure (IntScalar → OrderedRing
//      / Semiring per RustIntKind; FloatScalar →
//      ApproximateField; BoolScalar → BooleanAlgebra; CharScalar
//      → Cardinality refinement on Int32 (the surrogate-excluded
//      Unicode scalar value subset); StrScalar → FreeMonoid<Char>
//      per std/text.dag's UTF-8 framing; NeverScalar → empty
//      cardinality (the absurd-inhabitance type)). That grounding
//      IS the inhabitance walk owned ELSEWHERE per P2; the Disj
//      structure here is the closed surface, not a collapsing
//      target.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single Rust scalar value IS EXACTLY
//      ONE variant of this Disj; mutually-exclusive alternatives.
//      The shapes differ across variants (IntScalar carries two
//      coordinates; FloatScalar one; the rest none) — they cannot
//      be coordinates of a single record without the illegal-state
//      smell.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the six variants are NOT one
//      generic `F<X>` / projection over a separately-declared
//      source set (Practice 7). The PTX RegisterScalar pattern
//      (Conj-record over (kind, width, state_space)) was projection-
//      uniform; Rust scalars are NOT projection-uniform — they
//      have heterogeneous structural shapes (the kind partitions
//      across structurally-distinct value spaces, with three
//      distinct algebraic groundings + a cardinality refinement +
//      an empty-cardinality). The Disj-with-heterogeneous-payloads
//      is the irreducible faithful form.
// Terminal: the closed six-variant Rust primitive-scalar typed-value
// sum; illegal kind×width combinations are unrepresentable by
// construction; `()` is TupleType (Reference §3.10), out-of-scope
// for this slice.
type RustScalar
  = IntScalar { kind: RustIntKind, width: RustIntWidth }
  | FloatScalar { width: RustFloatWidth }
  | BoolScalar
  | CharScalar
  | StrScalar
  | NeverScalar


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// RustReferenceKind — the closed Rust reference-kind classifier. The
// Rust Reference §types-reference admits exactly two reference kinds
// (the borrow-kind partition that the borrow checker enforces):
// Spec: https://doc.rust-lang.org/reference/types/pointer.html
//
//   - Shared    — `&T`   shared / immutable reference; multiple
//                        concurrent shared references to the same
//                        place are admitted; the referent is
//                        read-only through the reference.
//   - Exclusive — `&mut T` exclusive / mutable reference; at most
//                        one exclusive reference to a given place
//                        exists at a time, and no shared reference
//                        coexists; the referent is read-write
//                        through the reference.
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). A third reference kind (e.g. a `&pin T`
// distinct from the existing `Pin<&T>` library wrapper) would be an
// edit to THIS declaration under the L-2 pin discipline. `Pin<&T>`
// itself is a LIBRARY (per L-2, libraries are just programs in the
// modeled language = Node), NOT a third reference kind.
//
// IN-B SEAM (effects intrinsic to the type signature, THESIS:401).
// The RustReference Conj record below ATTACHES this kind to an
// opaque lifetime Symbol, making the reference a structural Conj
// field on a typed value (the same shape as ptx.dag's
// `state_space: StateSpace` on RegisterScalar). The OWNERSHIP LENS
// (lens/ownership.dag, T-13) READS this field from typed
// signatures; the borrow-checker rules are a STRUCTURAL property
// of the signature, not a parallel ownership taxonomy.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every reference-bearing-typed-value
//      consumer (the ownership lens, the emission of `&` vs `&mut`,
//      the borrow-aliasing check) reads the kind tag uniformly;
//      shared classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: a `{ mutable: Bool }` proxy
//      replaces a closed 2-valued exhaustive sum with an unbounded
//      Bool (the same M4 smell as RustIntKind's signed-vs-unsigned),
//      AND conflates the BINDING-LEVEL mutability axis (`let mut`)
//      with the TYPE-LEVEL reference-kind axis (`&mut`) — those are
//      structurally distinct in the Rust Reference (place
//      expressions vs reference types). The enum IS the faithful
//      form on the reference-kind axis.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: the two reference
//      kinds do not trace to a richer std/ algebraic structure
//      (Shared / Exclusive are the borrow-kind partition; they
//      are not Lattice-ordered or algebra-inhabiting in their own
//      right — the algebras are on the REFERENT type, not the
//      reference). No richer std/ source.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single Rust reference has EXACTLY
//      ONE kind; `&T` and `&mut T` are alternatives, never
//      simultaneous coordinates (Rust forbids the co-existence at
//      runtime — that IS the borrow-checker's exclusivity rule).
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: Shared / Exclusive are the
//      Rust borrow-kind partition; they are NOT one generic
//      `F<X>` / projection over a separately-declared source set
//      (Practice 7). The kinds correspond to STRUCTURALLY DISTINCT
//      aliasing disciplines (many-reader-no-writer vs one-writer-
//      no-reader) — they are not projection-uniform over any
//      richer source.
// Terminal: the closed two-way Rust reference-kind partition; the
// borrow-checker's structural exclusivity invariant is what
// distinguishes the two, not a property the enum compresses.
type RustReferenceKind
  = Shared
  | Exclusive


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// NamedSegment — a module-identifier path segment. Conj record (no
// Practice-4 ledger).
//
//   - id    the segment's identifier (an opaque K-1 Symbol; the
//           spelling `outer_mod` / `inner_mod` etc. lives in the
//           boundary intern table, never readable from kernel /
//           lens code per node.dag:70-86)
//
// Used inside `PubInPath.suffix` for module-qualified visibility
// paths like `pub(in crate::outer_mod::inner_mod)`.
// Spec: https://doc.rust-lang.org/reference/paths.html
type NamedSegment {
  id: Symbol
}


// ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// PubInPath — a typed Rust `pub(in path)` resolved-scope carrier.
// Conj record (no Practice-4 ledger).
//
// CANONICAL SHAPE: an ABSOLUTE PATH FROM CRATE ROOT, expressed as a
// `List<NamedSegment>`. Empty list `[]` denotes the crate root itself
// (equivalent to `pub(crate)`); a non-empty list `[A, B, ...]` denotes
// the module at `crate::A::B::...`. ONE canonical PubInPath value per
// resolved ancestor scope — INVARIANTS P2 single-authority TYPE-
// ENFORCED.
//
// (Corrected 2026-05-16 per openai-pro/gpt-5.5-pro BLOCKING review
// 2026-05-16T08:36:04Z. Prior head-rooted shape
// `{ root: PubInRoot, suffix: List<NamedSegment> }` with PubInRoot =
// AtCrate | AtSuperChain admitted TWO structural values for the SAME
// resolved scope: `pub(super)` for an item in `crate::a::b` would
// canonicalize to `PubInPath { root: AtSuperChain { hops: OneSuper },
// suffix: [] }` while `pub(in crate::a)` for the same item would
// canonicalize to `PubInPath { root: AtCrate, suffix: [a] }` — both
// denoting the parent-module scope. Same resolved scope, two
// structural authorities = P2 violation. The earlier SuperHops +
// PubInRoot carriers were SOURCE-SPELLING artifacts — absolute-from-
// crate vs relative-from-current — not canonical forms. Per the
// openai-pro finding: the canonical carrier should key the resolved
// module once, not preserve absolute-vs-relative source spelling in
// the canonical value. Refactored to a single absolute-path-from-
// crate-root form; SuperHops + PubInRoot deleted as non-canonical. A
// future slice may want a separate pre-resolution syntactic carrier
// for IDE / diagnostic-span use cases; that lives alongside this one,
// not in place of it.)
//
//   - resolved_path   the absolute path FROM CRATE ROOT to the
//                     visibility-restriction module. Empty = crate
//                     root (= `pub(crate)`); `[a]` = `crate::a`;
//                     `[a, b, c]` = `crate::a::b::c`. The parser/
//                     resolver canonicalizes every valid `pub(in
//                     <SimplePath>)` source form to its absolute
//                     path before constructing this carrier.
//
// Reference's invalid-path cases — empty source path; non-ancestor
// modules; the SimplePath resolving to the item's current module
// (which is no-modifier visibility, canonicalizes to Private, not
// PubIn) — are parse/resolver-rejected or canonicalized upstream;
// the carrier represents only legitimate strict-ancestor canonical
// destinations + the crate-root case. P2 enforced structurally: a
// given resolved ancestor scope has EXACTLY ONE structural
// representation here (the absolute List<NamedSegment> from crate
// root to the target module).
// Spec: https://doc.rust-lang.org/reference/visibility-and-privacy.html
type PubInPath {
  resolved_path: List<NamedSegment>
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// PubInPath is POST-RESOLUTION canonical form (codex BLOCKING review
// 2026-05-16T06:26:18Z made this explicit).
//
// The Rust Reference SimplePath grammar admits multiple SOURCE forms
// that resolve to the same VISIBILITY SCOPE. PubInPath represents the
// post-canonicalization scope, NOT the source-path syntax. The parser /
// resolver is responsible for normalizing source forms into the
// canonical PubInPath / Private values before constructing
// RustVisibility. The carrier shape itself only admits canonical forms;
// non-canonical source paths cannot be constructed at all.
//
// Canonicalization rules (parser/resolver-side, per Reference's
// path-resolution rules):
//
//   SOURCE FORM                    →  CANONICAL RustVisibility
//   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//   <no `pub` modifier>            →  Private
//   `pub(self)`                    →  Private  (Reference equivalence)
//   `pub(in self)`                 →  Private  (Reference equivalence)
//   `pub`                          →  Pub
//   `pub(crate)` / `pub(in crate)` →  PubIn { path:
//                                     PubInPath { resolved_path: [] } }
//                                     (empty absolute path = crate root)
//                                     — APPLIES ONLY when the item is
//                                     declared in a STRICT DESCENDANT
//                                     of the crate root (i.e. inside
//                                     some `crate::a::b::…` module).
//                                     For an item declared AT THE
//                                     CRATE ROOT itself (top-level
//                                     item in `lib.rs` / `main.rs`),
//                                     the crate-root visibility scope
//                                     coincides with the no-modifier
//                                     scope: Private = "defining
//                                     module + descendants" = "crate
//                                     root + all of crate" = same as
//                                     pub(crate). For crate-root items,
//                                     `pub(crate)` canonicalizes to
//                                     `Private` (single authority for
//                                     the no-modifier scope). This is
//                                     the same context-dependent rule
//                                     as the `pub(in <current-module>)`
//                                     reflexive-ancestor case, applied
//                                     to the crate-root-as-current-
//                                     module special case (operator
//                                     BLOCKING 2026-05-16T08:36:04Z +
//                                     openai-pro REQUEST_CHANGES
//                                     2026-05-16T09:27:05Z).
//   `pub(in <SimplePath>)`         →  the resolver computes the
//                                     ABSOLUTE PATH from crate root to
//                                     the module the SimplePath
//                                     resolves to, then:
//                                       — resolved module IS a STRICT
//                                         ancestor of the item   →
//                                         PubIn { path: PubInPath {
//                                           resolved_path:
//                                             <absolute-from-crate-root>
//                                         } }
//                                       — resolved module IS the item's
//                                         current module (reflexive
//                                         ancestor)              →
//                                         Private (the no-modifier
//                                         scope; same canonical form
//                                         as `pub(self)` / no-modifier
//                                         per Reference equivalence)
//                                       — resolved module is NOT an
//                                         ancestor of the item     →
//                                         parse/resolver REJECTS
//                                         (per Reference's ancestor
//                                         rule for `pub(in path)`)
//                                     Concretely, for an item declared
//                                     in `crate::a::b::c`:
//                                       `pub(super)`              →
//                                         resolves to `crate::a::b`
//                                         → PubIn { path: PubInPath {
//                                           resolved_path: [a, b] } }
//                                       `pub(super::super)`       →
//                                         resolves to `crate::a`
//                                         → PubIn { path: PubInPath {
//                                           resolved_path: [a] } }
//                                       `pub(in crate::a)`        →
//                                         resolves to `crate::a`
//                                         → PubIn { path: PubInPath {
//                                           resolved_path: [a] } }
//                                         (SAME canonical value as
//                                         `pub(super::super)` — single
//                                         authority per resolved scope)
//                                       `pub(in crate::a::b::c)`  →
//                                         resolves to current module
//                                         → Private (reflexive case)
//                                       `pub(in self::super)`     →
//                                         resolves to `crate::a::b`
//                                         → SAME as `pub(super)`
//                                       `pub(in crate::other)`    →
//                                         not an ancestor → REJECTED
//   `pub(in $crate::…)` (macro     →  out-of-scope for this slice
//   context)                          (SCOPE NEGATIVE per the header;
//                                     hygienic-macro support is a
//                                     future C1 edit to NamedSegment /
//                                     a sibling macro-context carrier).
//
// SINGLE-AUTHORITY INVARIANT (BLOCKING operator review 2026-05-16T07:
// 30:48Z + openai-pro REQUEST_CHANGES 2026-05-16T08:36:04Z). Multiple
// source forms with the SAME resolved module MUST canonicalize to the
// SAME RustVisibility value. The absolute-path-from-crate-root form
// makes this STRUCTURALLY enforced: there is exactly ONE
// `List<NamedSegment>` per module under crate. The resolver does the
// path-resolution work (walk up super-chains, look up self-prefix
// paths, etc.) before constructing PubInPath; the carrier doesn't
// distinguish source-spelling variants because there's no place to
// put that distinction.
//
// The carrier provides the destination type; the parser/resolver
// provides the canonicalization. Source-syntax modeling for IDE /
// diagnostic-span use cases is an explicit future-substrate question
// — a separate pre-resolution syntactic carrier would live alongside
// PubInPath, not in place of it.


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// RustVisibility — the closed Rust item-visibility classifier. The
// Rust Reference §visibility-and-privacy partitions item visibility
// into TWO fundamental forms — unrestricted (`pub`) and path-
// restricted (`pub(in SimplePath)`) — with `pub(crate)` / `pub(self)`
// / `pub(super)` as syntactic CONVENIENCE FORMS of the latter
// (Reference: "`pub(crate)` is the same as `pub(in crate)`. `pub(super)`
// is the same as `pub(in super)`. `pub(self)` is the same as
// `pub(in self)` AND is the same as not using `pub` at all"). The
// faithful structural partition is therefore THREE variants — the
// absent / unrestricted / path-restricted axis — with the path-
// restricted variant CARRYING its SimplePath as a head-rooted
// `PubInPath { resolved_path: List<NamedSegment> }` (declared above
// this type). The carrier is keyed by the ABSOLUTE PATH FROM CRATE
// ROOT to the resolved ancestor module — ONE canonical PubInPath
// value per resolved scope, P2 single-authority TYPE-ENFORCED.
// Reference-invalid path-qualifier orderings are upstream parse-
// rejected by the resolver before construction. All valid source
// forms (`pub(self)` / `pub(in self)` / no-modifier / `pub(crate)` /
// `pub(in crate)` / `pub(super)` / `pub(in super::…)` / `pub(in
// self::super::…)` / `pub(in <SimplePath>)`) canonicalize to either
// Private (if resolved scope is the item's current module) or PubIn
// { path: PubInPath { resolved_path: <absolute-from-crate-root> } }
// (if resolved scope is a strict ancestor) per the canonicalization
// rules table below the PubInPath declaration. Multiple source forms
// with the SAME resolved scope produce the SAME canonical value.
// Per BLOCKING reviews 2026-05-16T05:06:51Z / 06:26:18-19Z /
// 07:30:47-48Z / 08:36:04Z (openai-pro REQUEST_CHANGES).
// Spec: https://doc.rust-lang.org/reference/visibility-and-privacy.html
//
//   - Private — items without a `pub` modifier (the Rust default;
//               visible only within the defining module and its
//               descendants per the privacy rules). PER REFERENCE
//               EQUIVALENCE: this variant is ALSO the canonical
//               representation of `pub(self)` and `pub(in self)`
//               (the Reference states all three are the same
//               visibility). The three source forms (no-modifier,
//               `pub(self)`, `pub(in self)`) ALL canonicalize to
//               THIS variant — STRUCTURALLY: PubInPath carries an
//               absolute-from-crate-root path; the resolver
//               canonicalizes any source form whose resolved
//               module IS the item's current module to Private
//               (not PubIn). One canonical representation per
//               resolved scope; the no-modifier scope has exactly
//               one structural form: Private.
//   - Pub     — `pub`         publicly visible (no restriction
//               beyond the defining crate's API surface)
//   - PubIn { path: PubInPath }
//             — `pub(in SimplePath)` — visible to the module at
//               the resolved SimplePath. The path is
//               `PubInPath { resolved_path: List<NamedSegment> }`
//               (declared above this type) — an ABSOLUTE PATH FROM
//               CRATE ROOT. Empty resolved_path denotes the crate
//               root itself (= `pub(crate)`); non-empty denotes
//               the module at `crate::<seg1>::<seg2>::…`.
//               Canonical examples (for an item in `crate::a::b::c`):
//               `pub(crate)` → `PubIn { path: { resolved_path: [] } }`;
//               `pub(super)` → `PubIn { path: { resolved_path: [a, b] } }`;
//               `pub(super::super)` → `PubIn { path: { resolved_path: [a] } }`;
//               `pub(in crate::a)` → SAME as `pub(super::super)`
//               (same resolved scope → same canonical value).
//               Source-syntax variants pointing at the SAME resolved
//               module produce the SAME canonical PubInPath value —
//               there is no source-spelling distinction in the
//               canonical form (P2 single-authority TYPE-ENFORCED
//               via the absolute-from-crate-root shape). Reference-
//               invalid path-qualifier orderings (a non-ancestor
//               resolved module; an empty SimplePath) are parse/
//               resolver-rejected before construction. Self-prefixed
//               source forms (`pub(in self::super)`, etc.) resolve
//               via name-resolution to their absolute ancestor path
//               before construction; the carrier doesn't see the
//               source-spelling prefix. `pub(self)` / `pub(in self)`
//               / `pub(in <current-module-path>)` all canonicalize
//               to Private (the no-modifier scope; one canonical
//               representation).
//
// (Corrected 2026-05-16 per THREE BLOCKING reviews on PR #3174:
//   1. Prior 4-way partition `Private | Crate | Super | Pub` dropped
//      the `pub(in SimplePath)` path fact (P1 + P2 violation).
//   2. Folding ALL `pub(in path)` syntactic forms into a
//      `PubIn { path: List<Symbol> }` included `pub(in self)` /
//      `pub(self)` — both Reference-equivalent to NO-MODIFIER
//      (Private), so the model admitted TWO authorities for the
//      same visibility scope: `Private` AND
//      `PubIn { path: [self-symbol] }` (P2 violation).
//   3. The remediation in fix 2 documented the invariant as a
//      producer obligation only (`rust_visibility_well_formed`
//      noop fn / doc-comment) — but the structural state
//      `PubIn { path: [self-symbol] }` was still CONSTRUCTIBLE
//      (codex REQUEST_CHANGES 2026-05-16T02:51:57Z: "the
//      single-authority rule for pub(self)/pub(in self) vs Private
//      is not structurally enforced by the model being introduced
//      here"). The illegal-states-unrepresentable discipline
//      (Practices 2 & 6) requires STRUCTURAL enforcement.
//   4. The first round-3 correction introduced `RustPathSegment`
//      (Crate | Super | Named) and `PubIn { path:
//      List<RustPathSegment> }` — the Self exclusion was type-
//      enforced, but the list itself remained UNCONSTRAINED, so
//      illegal Reference path-qualifier orderings were still
//      constructible: `[]` (empty), `[Crate, Super]` (super after
//      crate root; no parent of crate), `[Named]` alone (bare named
//      without root), and `Super` after a `Named` segment (e.g.
//      `[Crate, Named, Super]` = `crate::foo::super`, an invalid
//      super-position). Operator BLOCKING 2026-05-16T05:06:51Z
//      named this: "PubIn stores an unconstrained
//      List<RustPathSegment>, so impossible or edition-dependent
//      visibility paths […] are constructible despite Rust
//      Reference path-qualifier rules, violating extdeps fidelity
//      and INVARIANTS P2."
//   5. Round-4 introduced a head-rooted Conj
//      `PubInPath { root: PubInRoot, suffix: List<NamedSegment> }`
//      with PubInRoot = AtCrate | AtSuperChain { hops: SuperHops }.
//      Reference-invalid orderings were structurally unrepresentable
//      under that shape, but it admitted TWO structural values for
//      the SAME resolved scope: `pub(super)` for an item in
//      `crate::a::b` → `PubInPath { root: AtSuperChain { hops:
//      OneSuper }, suffix: [] }`, while `pub(in crate::a)` for the
//      same item → `PubInPath { root: AtCrate, suffix: [a] }`.
//      Both denoted the parent-module scope; same resolved scope,
//      two structural authorities = P2 violation. Operator BLOCKING
//      2026-05-16T07:30:48Z + openai-pro REQUEST_CHANGES
//      2026-05-16T08:36:04Z named this: "the canonical carrier
//      should key the resolved module once, not preserve absolute-
//      vs-relative source spelling in the canonical value."
// The final corrected partition collapses to a single
// absolute-path-from-crate-root shape: `PubInPath { resolved_path:
// List<NamedSegment> }` (declared above this type). Empty
// `resolved_path` = the crate root (≡ `pub(crate)`); non-empty =
// `crate::<seg1>::<seg2>::…`. ONE canonical PubInPath value per
// resolved ancestor scope — P2 single-authority TYPE-ENFORCED via
// the absolute shape (no parallel absolute-vs-relative forms to
// pick between). SuperHops + PubInRoot are DELETED as non-
// canonical source-spelling artifacts; the resolver canonicalizes
// every valid `pub(in <SimplePath>)` source form to its absolute
// path before constructing PubInPath. Reference-invalid orderings
// (non-ancestor resolved modules) are parse/resolver-rejected
// upstream; the reflexive-current-module case canonicalizes to
// Private (one canonical representation for the no-modifier
// scope). The earlier doc wording that claimed `self::outer`
// "canonicalizes by dropping the leading self" was WRONG —
// corrected per BLOCKING operator review 2026-05-16T03:44:50Z; the
// new absolute-path shape moots the self-prefix question entirely
// (the resolver computes the absolute resolved path, regardless of
// source-syntax prefix).)
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). A fourth top-level form is an edit to
// THIS declaration under the L-2 pin discipline. The PubIn variant
// carries the path STRUCTURALLY (not as a separate parallel-named
// variant), so future Reference revisions adding new convenience
// shorthand for an existing path family are NOT a C1 edit — they
// fold into PubIn's path-list.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every Rust-item consumer (emission
//      of the visibility modifier, the cross-module-name-resolution
//      walk, the public-API-surface synthesis for documentation /
//      coordination contracts) reads the visibility tag uniformly;
//      shared classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: collapsing to a uniform record
//      `{ kind: Symbol, path: List<Symbol>? }` reintroduces the
//      readable-string-as-tag heuristic K-1 forecloses AND the
//      illegal state "kind=Pub with path=[...]" (Pub carries no
//      path — that combination is unrepresentable here by
//      construction; the Disj-with-payloads is the dissolved form
//      per P2 + Practice 2/6 + M4).
//   3. Algebraic form — PARTIAL: visibility scopes form a partial
//      order (Private ⊑ PubIn { path: [Super] } ⊑ PubIn { path:
//      [Crate] } ⊑ Pub for items in a nested module — and PubIn
//      paths in general are ordered by module containment;
//      pub(self) ≡ Private is structurally enforced, not a
//      separate level). The partial-order is DERIVED FROM the
//      closed enum + the namespace's module-containment relation,
//      not a richer std/ source — pointing to a
//      `BoundedLattice<RustVisibility>` would parallel-represent
//      the visibility taxonomy the order is defined over
//      (algebra.dag U1 engine-debt smell — the same argument
//      ptx.dag's BarrierScope ledger makes).
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single Rust item has EXACTLY ONE
//      visibility modifier; the three variants are mutually-
//      exclusive alternatives (an item is private, OR `pub`, OR
//      `pub(in <path>)` — never a combination). The path-INSIDE
//      PubIn is a structural Conj field within that variant, not
//      a separate Disj coordinate.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the three variants are NOT
//      one generic `F<X>` / projection over a separately-declared
//      source set (Practice 7). Private and Pub are nullary
//      tags; PubIn carries a PubInPath (absolute-from-crate-root
//      `{ resolved_path: List<NamedSegment> }`). They are
//      structurally heterogeneous (the Disj-with-payloads shape
//      is irreducible — collapsing PubIn into Private/Pub would
//      lose the path fact, exactly the regression this BLOCKING
//      fix undid).
// Terminal: the closed three-way Rust item-visibility partition
// reflecting the Reference's fundamental form. Reference
// equivalences canonicalize to absolute-from-crate-root paths:
// `pub(crate)` / `pub(in crate)` → `PubIn { path: PubInPath {
// resolved_path: [] } }`; `pub(super)` for item in `crate::a::b`
// → `PubIn { path: PubInPath { resolved_path: [a] } }` (the
// parent module's absolute path); `pub(self)` / `pub(in self)` /
// no-modifier / any `pub(in <path>)` resolving to current module
// → `Private` (one canonical representation for the no-modifier
// scope).
type RustVisibility
  = Private
  | Pub
  | PubIn { path: PubInPath }


// RustVisibility well-formedness — TYPE-ENFORCED via PubInPath's
// absolute-from-crate-root single-canonical-form discipline.
//
// The single-authority invariant "same resolved scope ⇒ same
// RustVisibility value" is structurally enforced by PubInPath's
// shape: there is exactly ONE `List<NamedSegment>` per module
// under crate (the absolute path from crate root), so every valid
// `pub(in <SimplePath>)` source form canonicalizes to a unique
// PubInPath value at the resolver. Source-spelling variants (e.g.
// `pub(super)` vs `pub(in crate::a)` for an item in `crate::a::b`)
// resolve to the SAME absolute path and produce the SAME canonical
// PubInPath value; the carrier has no place to encode source-
// spelling distinctions. Reference's reflexive-current-module case
// canonicalizes to Private (one structural representation for the
// no-modifier scope). Illegal-non-ancestor and empty-path cases are
// parse/resolver-rejected upstream; the carrier admits only valid
// strict-ancestor + crate-root destinations. Illegal states
// unrepresentable per modeling-discipline.md §Practices 2/6 AND
// INVARIANTS P2; NOT a doc-only producer obligation, NOT a noop
// well-formedness fn.


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// RustReference — a typed Rust reference. Per Reference §types-pointer,
// a reference type is `&'lifetime mut? TypeNoBounds` — THREE structural
// coordinates: lifetime × reference-kind × referent type. All three
// are CARRIED here so `&'a i32` and `&'a str` are distinct facts
// (P1 modeling-faithfulness + P2 boundary-discipline). Conj record
// (no Practice-4 ledger — only coproducts dissolve).
// Spec: https://doc.rust-lang.org/reference/types/pointer.html
//
//   - kind          which reference kind (Shared / Exclusive — the
//                   borrow-kind partition the borrow checker reads;
//                   the `mut?` slot in the Reference grammar)
//   - lifetime      the reference's lifetime identity (an opaque
//                   K-1 Symbol — equality only, no spelling
//                   accessor; the human-readable `'a` / `'static`
//                   spellings live in a boundary intern table, never
//                   in kernel/lens code, exactly as Symbol's K-1
//                   discipline forecloses — node.dag:70-86; the
//                   `'lifetime` slot in the Reference grammar)
//   - referent      the type the reference points to (the Reference's
//                   `TypeNoBounds` slot). Carried as a `Node` — the
//                   bounded kernel's single recursive type per
//                   node.dag A1 ("recursion lives SOLELY in Node
//                   children") — same shape as `Edge.target: Node`
//                   and `Diagnostic.correction Suggested(Node)`: any
//                   type expression IS a Node (a subtree of Nodes
//                   IS a Node; there is no separate "TypeNoBounds
//                   alias" the compiler sees through, per
//                   feedback_nodes_are_nodes /
//                   feedback_naming_is_aliasing). Whether the
//                   referent is in fact a TypeNode (a kind=TypeNode
//                   Node) is a structural well-formedness invariant
//                   the LanguageModel grammar enforces — the same
//                   discipline node.dag's `node_well_formed`
//                   applies to a connective's child shapes, not a
//                   type-level constraint here.
//
// (Corrected 2026-05-16 per BLOCKING operator review on PR #3174:
// the prior `{ kind, lifetime }`-only shape dropped the referent
// fact, so `&'a i32` and `&'a str` collapsed to one structural
// fact. INVARIANTS P1 modeling-faithfulness AND P2 boundary-
// discipline violations — the Reference's ReferenceType rule
// CARRIES all three coordinates. Faithful form restored.)
//
// The `lifetime` Symbol IS the generic-lifetime-parameter surface
// the bundled T-4 LanguageModel grammar productions resolve. A
// concrete lifetime ('a, 'static, an anonymous-region from elision)
// resolves to a Symbol at the binding site (per node.dag's name-
// reference resolution through the namespace); the Conj record is
// the typed value the ownership lens reads.
//
// IN-B / structural-fact discipline (per the header STRUCTURAL
// FINDING). The RustReference Conj record IS the structural surface
// of the IN-B claim "ownership / lifetime are intrinsic to the type
// signature, NOT an annotation layer" (THESIS:401, scaffold header
// IN-B framing applied to Rust). Memory access through a reference
// is a Transform whose operand type CARRIES this record in its
// signature; the ownership lens (lens/ownership.dag, T-13) READS
// the (kind, lifetime, referent) coordinates directly from the
// typed signature and validates well-scopedness as a structural
// property — there is no separate "borrow-fact annotation" layer.
// The referent's own type-Node structure is recursively read by the
// lens as needed (since `referent: Node` admits the full type-
// expression grammar — array types, generic instantiations, function
// pointer types, further references — without this file declaring
// each shape separately; that grammar is the bundled T-4
// LanguageModel work, this Conj is the carrier shape).
type RustReference {
  kind: RustReferenceKind
  lifetime: Symbol
  referent: Node
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// RustCost — the per-target cost-realization carrier shape for the
// Rust `LanguageModel`. Conj record (no Practice-4 ledger).
// Spec: gunbc-internal — cost realization is a v4 substrate concern
// (U1 / U2 per std/algebra.dag + lens/cost.dag), not a Rust-language
// spec fact; no upstream URL applies. The carrier SHAPE is gunbc's,
// pinned by the U1 one-homomorphism-with-cost discipline.
//
// This declares the SHAPE the U1 spine reads when computing Rust-
// side realization cost — both components per the scaffold header's
// "cost realization: Rust-specific bounds for compositional cost
// lens" claim (preserved as a goal; the carrier shape lands now,
// the cost INSTANCE-VALUES land downstream per "Owned ELSEWHERE"):
//
//   - instruction_cost   per-operation throughput cost (the U1
//                        per-target-op cost projection — the `map`
//                        side of `Homomorphism<…, …, RustScalar>`
//                        carries this; cost is read FROM the map's
//                        structure, never stored as a parallel
//                        field per algebra.dag's U1 no-engine
//                        discipline)
//   - allocation_cost    per-allocation cost (the Rust-specific
//                        contribution: heap allocations through
//                        `Box` / `Vec` / `String` carry a cost
//                        distinct from per-instruction throughput;
//                        the per-target shape that the cost lens
//                        T-12 reads when synthesizing a Rust
//                        realization's total cost)
//
// 🟡 SCAFFOLD (same bridge as std/diagnostic.dag's `Extent.ByteRange`
// and ptx.dag's `Dim3` / `PtxCost`). With only kernel-ambient `Int`
// available at the T-4 phase, `{ instruction_cost: Int,
// allocation_cost: Int }` STRUCTURALLY ADMITS illegal states
// (negative costs; nonsense magnitudes). modeling-discipline.md §2
// (illegal-states-unrepresentable) forbids admitting them; no
// bounded-Cardinality refinement substrate exists yet to exclude
// them. It is a TRACKED scaffold, declared with the three bridge
// properties (the precedent diagnostic.dag established 2026-05-15):
//   (a) scaffold doc — THIS note; the carrier is provisional, not
//       the terminal refined-positive shape.
//   (b) bounds on use — a producer MUST construct only non-negative
//       costs; until the substrate enforces it the invariant is
//       producer-side; a RustCost with negative components is a
//       producer bug, not a legal value.
//   (c) dissolution trigger — when std/cardinality.dag's refinement
//       substrate lands (T-3, P4 decidability), `instruction_cost`
//       and `allocation_cost` become refined bounded-non-negative-
//       Int axes, and this scaffold dissolves.
//
// SEAM (where T-12 cost-lens reads). When lens/cost.dag (T-12, U2)
// lands, its `SymbolicCost` lattice carrier composes over the per-
// target projections; the Rust projection is read from this Conj
// record's components per the U2 "complexity consumes cost, never
// re-derives" discipline. Pre-T-12, RustCost is the declared SHAPE
// the projection plugs into; concrete cost instance-values per
// operation (e.g. `Add<Int>` costs `RustCost { instruction_cost: 1,
// allocation_cost: 0 }`) ride T-12 + D2 per "Owned ELSEWHERE."
type RustCost {
  instruction_cost: Int
  allocation_cost: Int
}


// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// Per-primitive named registry + IrToRust mapping shape.
//
// The two halves of the IR ↔ Rust connection (operator-directed
// 2026-05-16, conversational ratification — the SHAPE goes in this
// PR; the connecting rows land as their `ir_carrier` std/ types
// reach merged state).
//
//   (1) Per-primitive named inhabitants — each Rust primitive type
//       gets a stable named identity as a `data X: RustScalar = ...`
//       closed-enum value. These are STRUCTURAL FACTS (not algebra-
//       inhabitance — no D2 trap; just typed values of the closed
//       RustScalar Disj). emit / ingest dispatch on these by
//       STRUCTURAL identity, never on a spelling.
//
//   (2) IrToRust Conj carrier — names a single IR-type ↔ Rust-type
//       correspondence row. emit (T-10) reads these rows; ingest
//       (the C5 reverse direction) walks them backwards. The
//       carrier SHAPE lands now; the row instances ride T-3
//       (each row's `ir_carrier` references a std/ type Node that
//       must exist in the namespace before the row can resolve).
//
// This is the SEAM through which all IR → Rust emission flows.
// One row per std/ ↔ Rust primitive correspondence; emit dispatches
// by structural-identity match on `ir_carrier`, never by name.


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Per-primitive named inhabitants — 18 typed scalar values, one per
// Rust primitive type. Each is a `data` instance of `RustScalar`'s
// closed-enum payload-bearing variants; the `(kind, width)`
// combinations are STRUCTURALLY enforced by RustScalar's Disj-with-
// payloads (Bool/Char/Str/Never carry no width; only Int/Float do).
//
// These are NOT algebra-inhabitance instance-values (D2-pending) —
// they are just typed VALUES of the closed Disj. Authorable today;
// fan-out to python/go/cpp/typescript mirrors this shape.

// Signed integers — `i8` / `i16` / `i32` / `i64` / `i128` / `isize`
data rust_i8:    RustScalar = IntScalar { kind: Signed,   width: Bits8   }
data rust_i16:   RustScalar = IntScalar { kind: Signed,   width: Bits16  }
data rust_i32:   RustScalar = IntScalar { kind: Signed,   width: Bits32  }
data rust_i64:   RustScalar = IntScalar { kind: Signed,   width: Bits64  }
data rust_i128:  RustScalar = IntScalar { kind: Signed,   width: Bits128 }
data rust_isize: RustScalar = IntScalar { kind: Signed,   width: Pointer }

// Unsigned integers — `u8` / `u16` / `u32` / `u64` / `u128` / `usize`
data rust_u8:    RustScalar = IntScalar { kind: Unsigned, width: Bits8   }
data rust_u16:   RustScalar = IntScalar { kind: Unsigned, width: Bits16  }
data rust_u32:   RustScalar = IntScalar { kind: Unsigned, width: Bits32  }
data rust_u64:   RustScalar = IntScalar { kind: Unsigned, width: Bits64  }
data rust_u128:  RustScalar = IntScalar { kind: Unsigned, width: Bits128 }
data rust_usize: RustScalar = IntScalar { kind: Unsigned, width: Pointer }

// IEEE-754 floating-point — `f32` / `f64` (stable Rust set)
data rust_f32:   RustScalar = FloatScalar { width: Bits32 }
data rust_f64:   RustScalar = FloatScalar { width: Bits64 }

// Reference-primitive scalars — `bool` / `char` / `str` / `!`
data rust_bool:  RustScalar = BoolScalar
data rust_char:  RustScalar = CharScalar
data rust_str:   RustScalar = StrScalar
data rust_never: RustScalar = NeverScalar


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// IrToRust<IRCarrier> — the IR-type ↔ Rust-primitive correspondence
// carrier, parameterized over the IR-side type. One row per std/
// canonical carrier ↔ Rust primitive emission. Conj record (no
// Practice-4 ledger).
// Spec: this carrier IS the U1 "coercion = emission" projection
//       (algebra.dag U1 header) made concrete per language. Same
//       shape per-language across fan-out.
//
// IRCarrier (type-parameter)  the std/ canonical type this row maps
//                             to/from (a type-level reference — e.g.
//                             `v4.std.logic.Bool` or
//                             `v4.std.nat.Nat`). Each row's IR side
//                             is named at the TYPE level, not as a
//                             value field — same shape as
//                             `algebra.dag`'s
//                             `Homomorphism<C, Source, Target>` which
//                             carries the algebra class C and the
//                             two carriers at the type-parameter
//                             positions. Type-level keying is the
//                             U1-canonical form; emit dispatches by
//                             type-identity match on IRCarrier,
//                             never by name (K-1).
//   - rust_repr               the Rust-side typed scalar value — one
//                             of the 18 per-primitive named
//                             inhabitants above.
//
// SEAM (where rows land). Each row's `IrToRust<IRCarrier>` type
// instantiation requires IRCarrier to be a declared std/ type. As
// the T-3 wave merges land std/ scalar types, the corresponding
// `IrToRust<...>` rows land here. The row authoring is mechanical —
// one per std/-↔-Rust correspondence — and incremental: a row can
// land the moment its IRCarrier std/ type reaches merged state,
// without waiting for the whole std/ wave.
//
// Worked example: `Bool → rust_bool` (concrete, LANDED below — see
// `data bool_to_rust_bool` row after this declaration). emit (T-10)
// consumes the row:
//   given a Bool-typed value in InferredTree
//   →  lookup `bool_to_rust_bool` by type-identity match on the
//      Bool IRCarrier (compile-time-typed; no runtime string match)
//   →  read `rust_repr` = `rust_bool` = `BoolScalar`
//   →  emit Rust source token `"bool"` (the spelling determined by
//      the RustScalar's variant — BoolScalar maps to "bool" via the
//      LanguageModel grammar)
// ingest (reverse direction, C5) walks the row backwards:
//   parsed Rust `bool` keyword
//   →  produces BoolScalar Node
//   →  reverse-lookup: rust_repr == rust_bool ⇒ Bool IRCarrier
//   →  Bool-typed Node into the IR
//
// The whole IR ↔ Rust pipeline IS one declarative correspondence
// table consumed in both directions. No name-convention dispatch,
// no string matching — type-level identity match on IRCarrier.
type IrToRust<IRCarrier> {
  rust_repr: RustScalar
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Bool → rust_bool — the first concrete IR ↔ Rust mapping row.
//
// std/logic.dag declares `Bool = True | False` (a closed Disj per
// the classical-bivalent logic anchor) and the
// `bool_boolean_algebra: BooleanAlgebra<Bool>` inhabitance. Rust's
// `bool` per Reference §types-boolean is the same two-valued type
// inhabiting the same BooleanAlgebra; the IR ↔ Rust correspondence
// is 1:1, and this row encodes it.
//
// Spec (IR side):   std/logic.dag (single authority; its own
//                   `Anchor:` line cites the upstream Boolean-algebra
//                   reference — don't duplicate that anchor here)
// Spec (Rust side): https://doc.rust-lang.org/reference/types/boolean.html
//
// post-T-3-Wave-A1 (std/logic.dag merged 2026-05-16): this row is
// now AUTHORED (not commented). emit (T-10) reads it; ingest (C5)
// reads it backwards. When the LanguageModel grammar lands the Rust
// source spelling for BoolScalar (the token `"bool"`), the
// end-to-end correspondence is complete: a `Bool`-typed `.dag`
// value emits to Rust source as `bool`, and Rust source `bool`
// ingests back to a `Bool`-typed Node.
//
// (This is the answer to the operator's conversational question
// 2026-05-16: "can we actually write testcases for the IR <---> rust
// side now?" — for Bool, yes, the SHAPE is now ready. The
// TestClaim itself awaits std/verification.dag's TestClaim schema,
// pending in the same T-3 Wave-A2.)
data bool_to_rust_bool: IrToRust<Bool> = IrToRust {
  rust_repr: rust_bool
}


// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// Layer-2 structural-assertion fns (the per-row "expectation" pattern
// every language file mirrors during fan-out).
//
// v4 testing has THREE layers, with the gating dependencies named:
//
//   Layer 1 — 0-diag structural typing (live now, every PR).
//     Every `data X: T = ...` instance is itself a type-check
//     assertion v2-compiler verifies. The row
//     `data bool_to_rust_bool: IrToRust<Bool> = IrToRust { rust_repr:
//     rust_bool }` already proves: Bool resolves, IrToRust<Bool> is a
//     well-typed instantiation, rust_bool is a real RustScalar value,
//     and the Conj record matches the carrier's shape. Drift breaks
//     the build. Not a "test" in the TestClaim sense, but real
//     structural verification.
//
//   Layer 2 — Structural-assertion fns (the pattern below, AUTHORABLE
//     NOW). A `fn assert_<row>_consistency() -> Bool` exhaustive-
//     matches on the row's `rust_repr` field and asserts which
//     RustScalar variant it MUST be. v2-compiler's exhaustiveness
//     check ensures all variants are covered; the fn body encodes
//     the expected variant. Today the fn compiles via 0-diag (the
//     match arms type-check); the BEHAVIORAL fact (the fn returns
//     `true` when the row is correct) becomes verifiable when an
//     interpreter exists (T-22 eval) or when a TestClaim with
//     `kind: Equals` asserts the return value (T-3 Wave-A2 +
//     std/verification.dag, PR #3183 in flight).
//
//     The fn is a FORWARD-ANCHOR: it documents the expected variant
//     as a structural property (a reader pattern-matching against
//     each variant sees the canonical answer), and when TestClaim
//     infrastructure lands, the fn body becomes the `expected`
//     side of an `Equals` TestClaim. If a future PR changes
//     `bool_to_rust_bool` to point at `rust_i32` instead of
//     `rust_bool`, the fn still compiles (the match exhausts) but
//     RETURNS `false` — flag-when-runnable.
//
//   Layer 3 — TestClaim data (gate: PR #3183 std/verification.dag).
//     The real "tests" in the v4 self-hosting sense are TestClaim
//     instances:
//
//       data t_bool_to_rust_bool_roundtrip: TestClaim = TestClaim {
//         kind:     RoundTrips,
//         label:    "IR Bool ↔ Rust bool roundtrips",
//         input:    <Bool-typed Node>,
//         expected: <... same Node after emit + parse roundtrip ...>
//       }
//
//     RoundTrips claims need emit (T-10) + parse (T-7) — both also
//     gated. The DECLARATIVE TestClaim data is the first layer that
//     unlocks the moment #3183 merges; EXECUTABLE roundtrips need
//     #3183 + T-7 + T-10.
//
// Per-language fan-out (python.dag / go.dag / cpp.dag / typescript.dag):
//   Each language file mirrors this layer-2 shape. Their per-primitive
//   Bool row will have an analogous `assert_bool_to_<lang>_bool_consistency`
//   fn matching on their language's bool variant. The PATTERN is
//   identical; only the carrier names change. Authoring this fn for
//   Rust BEFORE fan-out establishes the template every fan-out worker
//   copies, removing one decision-point from their slice.


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// assert_bool_to_rust_bool_consistency — Layer-2 structural assertion
// for the live Bool → rust_bool IrToRust row.
//
// Verifies via exhaustive match that `bool_to_rust_bool.rust_repr` IS
// the `BoolScalar` variant, not any other RustScalar kind. If a future
// edit changes the row to use a different per-primitive inhabitant
// (e.g. `rust_i32`), the fn still compiles (the match exhausts) but
// returns `false` — a structural change with a behavioral signal.
//
// Forward-anchor for the `Equals` TestClaim that will assert this fn
// returns `true` once T-3 Wave-A2 (std/verification.dag, PR #3183)
// merges:
//
//   // (post-#3183 merge — comment-only sketch:)
//   // data t_bool_to_rust_bool_kind: TestClaim = TestClaim {
//   //   kind:     Equals,
//   //   label:    "bool_to_rust_bool maps to BoolScalar variant",
//   //   input:    assert_bool_to_rust_bool_consistency(),
//   //   expected: true
//   // }
fn assert_bool_to_rust_bool_consistency() -> Bool {
  match bool_to_rust_bool.rust_repr {
    BoolScalar                       => true
    IntScalar { kind: _, width: _ }  => false
    FloatScalar { width: _ }         => false
    CharScalar                       => false
    StrScalar                        => false
    NeverScalar                      => false
  }
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// IrToRust row authoring — DEFERRED per row to the std/ carrier's
// merged state.
//
// Below is a comment-only authoring guide. Each row is independent:
// it lands as soon as its `ir_carrier` is declared in the relevant
// std/ file. The rows are NOT all-or-nothing — incremental landing
// is fine and recommended (one per PR if std/* lands incrementally).
//
// Status of std/ scalar carriers (snapshot 2026-05-16 post-T-3 merge):
//   - std/logic.dag (Bool)          ✅ LANDED (#3166)  → bool_to_rust_bool LANDED above
//   - std/nat.dag (Nat)             ✅ LANDED (#3165)  → needs std/integer.dag for u* mapping
//   - std/integer.dag (Int + widths) ❌ MISSING — unlocks rust_i* + rust_u* rows
//   - std/machine.dag (MachineWidth) ❌ MISSING — unlocks Pointer-width for rust_isize / rust_usize
//   - std/float.dag (Float)          ❌ MISSING — unlocks rust_f32 / rust_f64 rows
//   - std/text.dag (Char + Str)      ❌ MISSING — unlocks rust_char / rust_str rows
//
// As each std/ scalar lands, add a one-line `data <name>_to_<rust>:
// IrToRust<<Type>> = IrToRust { rust_repr: <rust_prim> }` row +
// the corresponding `import v4.std.<file> { <Type> }` line. v2-compiler
// verifies 0-diag at each landing (structural resolution check).
//
// Authoring template (use the live `bool_to_rust_bool` row above as
// the precedent — the IrToRust<IRCarrier> type-parameter form, NOT
// a value field):
//
//   import v4.std.<file> { <IRCarrier> }
//
//   data <ir>_to_<rust>: IrToRust<<IRCarrier>> = IrToRust {
//     rust_repr: <rust_primitive>
//   }
//
//   // Layer-2 assertion fn (forward-anchor for Equals TestClaim):
//   fn assert_<ir>_to_<rust>_consistency() -> Bool {
//     match <ir>_to_<rust>.rust_repr {
//       <expected RustScalar variant> => true
//       <all other variants>          => false
//     }
//   }
//
// Per-row TestClaim shape (pending std/verification.dag — PR #3183
// in flight; not yet merged):
//
//   data t_<ir>_to_<rust>_roundtrip: TestClaim = TestClaim {
//     kind:     RoundTrips,
//     label:    "IR <IRCarrier> ↔ Rust <rust> roundtrips through emit + ingest",
//     input:    <IRCarrier-typed Node>,
//     expected: <same Node — emit(input) → Rust-source-token,
//                ingest(token) → same Node by structural-identity>
//   }
//
// The TestClaim row IS the IR ↔ Rust testcase the operator asked
// about; it becomes authorable once std/verification.dag merges +
// the corresponding IrToRust row lands. The fully-instrumented
// testcase thus rides three landings: (a) std/<scalar>.dag, (b)
// this PR's shape (live for Bool now), (c) std/verification.dag's
// TestClaim schema (#3183).
